  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to improve upon the current binary representation by exploring non-binary values to potentially find lower C5 values. The binary representation might get stuck in local minima, while allowing values between 0 and 1 gives more flexibility. We use a multi-start optimization with stochastic rounding to generate initial configurations. This approach combines exploration with essential constraints to find better solutions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- n_points=280 samples over [0,2]\n- dx=2.0/280 (fixed by grid size)\n- Integral constraint: sum(h) * dx = 1\n- Objective: C5 = max_k \u222b h(x) * (1-h(x+k)) dx\n- Use stochastic rounding to convert continuous values to [0,1] while preserving integral constraint\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   a) Generate 16 seeded starts (1 parent + 15 stochastic variations)\n   b) Convert each start to binary with stochastic rounding\n   c) Run local optimization using SLSQP\n   d) Track best solution across all starts\n\n   Helper functions:\n   - `generate_watershed`: Create perturbed starts using:\n      1. Add noise (level=0.02)\n      2. Apply random shift (-20 to 20 samples)\n      3. Normalize to maintain integral constraint\n   - `random_starts(seed, n_starts=16, variation=True)`: Generate initial population\n   - `stochastic_round(x)`: Convert real to binary with probability based on value\n   - `objective_func(h)`: Compute C5 value and constraint\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - 16 fixed starts to explore solution space\n   - Noise level: 0.02 (reduced from 0.03 for better stability)\n   - Max iterations: 1500\n   - Ftol: 1e-8 (tighter tolerance)\n   - Feasibility repair: Linear rescaling to ensure integral constraint\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - 25 calls at 280 points \u2192 ~5-8s computation\n   - 80% chance of improvement with 100s budget\n   - Fallback: Current parent solution if no improvement\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n   - Check sum(h)*dx \u2248 1\n   - Compute exact C5 using correlation\n   - Return only binary solutions that satisfy constraints\n   - Maximize C5 (lower is better)\n\nDetailed steps:\n- Time tracking: Break after 100s\n- Solutions: Converted to binary using stochastic rounding\n- Metrics: Store best C5 across all evaluates\n- Bounds: Always (0,1) for each point\n- Constraints: Integral using exact equality",
